{
  "headline": {
    "id": "H1",
    "text": "Big Tech's AI Boom Shifts to Off-Balance-Sheet Debt and High Leverage Risks",
    "citations": []
  },
  "overview": [
    {
      "id": "O1",
      "text": "Big tech companies like Alphabet, Meta, and Amazon are increasingly using off-balance-sheet debt and lease commitments for their AI infrastructure projects, raising concerns about hidden financial risks.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_ref": "P1",
          "quote": "Big tech's AI boom is shifting to off-balance-sheet debt, lease deals, and high leverage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "O2",
      "text": "Goldman Sachs estimates that hyperscalers carry about $1.5 trillion in lease commitments, with roughly $1 trillion not yet recognized as conventional liabilities.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_ref": "P2",
          "quote": "Goldman Sachs has estimated that hyperscalers carry about $1.5 trillion in lease commitments. Roughly $1 trillion relates to leases that have not yet commenced and therefore are not recognized as conventional liabilities under U.S. accounting rules."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "key_points": [
    {
      "id": "K1",
      "text": "Meta’s Hyperion data center in Louisiana is an example of a joint venture structure that keeps debt off the balance sheet.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_ref": "P1",
          "quote": "The clearest example is Meta’s Hyperion data center in Louisiana. Rather than simply issue debt itself, Meta formed a joint venture with Blue Owl called Beignet, took a 20% stake and guaranteed that it would lease the facility for at least 20 years. That structure allowed Beignet to issue $27 billion of bonds while keeping the debt off Meta’s balance sheet."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "K2",
      "text": "The financialization of compute is creating new financial liabilities that are hard to classify as debt.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "passage_ref": "P2",
          "quote": "The next stage is the financialization of compute itself. NVIDIA Corporation and alternative-asset managers including Blackstone Inc. , BlackRock Inc. and KKR &amp; Co. Inc. are pursuing structures designed to treat chip capacity and computing contracts as investable assets."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No specific details on the exact amount of off-balance-sheet debt for each company.",
    "No information on the exact timeline for the shift to off-balance-sheet debt.",
    "No mention of the potential impact on stock prices or investor sentiment."
  ]
}

